Chapter 5 – How Programmers Work: Exploiting the Development Pipeline
A sloppy Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) isn’t just a process problem—it’s an attack surface. Every shortcut a team takes leaves fingerprints you can find and exploit. Here’s how each phase can leak vulnerabilities and how you, as a hunter, can spot them.

1. Design & Analysis → Missing Requirements = Missing Controls
If a feature wasn’t security-reviewed at design time, it ships with guesswork. Look for endpoints that behave inconsistently—like a password reset that doesn’t invalidate sessions because nobody thought of it.

Hunt tip: Map the feature’s logic; if it feels “bolted on,” test for privilege escalation. Design gaps often mean admin functions are reachable by lower-privileged users simply by guessing the URL.

2. Writing Code → Source Control Leaks
.git exposure: If /.git/HEAD or /.git/config is accessible, you can download the entire repository with tools like git-dumper. That gives you source code, hardcoded secrets, and commit history (which might show how old vulnerabilities were fixed—helping you reverse-engineer exploits).

Pull requests & code reviews: When reviews are skipped, bugs slip through. A common evidence: no branch protection on public repos, or open pull requests with sensitive info in comments. Even in private repos, misconfigured CI/CD can expose logs.

Hardcoded secrets: Developers often commit API keys, database passwords, or encryption keys. Search for TODO or FIXME as well—they point to unfinished code that probably isn’t secure.

3. Pre-Release Testing → Test Environments Are Gold Mines
Unsegregated staging: If you can reach a staging server (often staging.target.com or dev.target.com), it might have identical vulnerabilities but weaker security—no WAF, default passwords, verbose error messages.

Improperly scrubbed data: Test databases cloned from production can contain real user emails, bcrypt hashes you can crack offline, or even plaintext passwords. Find a staging endpoint that leaks data; it’s often an easy PII bounty.

Continuous integration leaks: Build logs in public CI services (e.g., Travis CI, GitHub Actions) may echo environment variables with secrets. Just scrolling through old build outputs can give you credentials.

4. The Release Process → Deploy Gone Wrong
Unreliable releases mean you might be hitting a mixed version of the application—an old endpoint still active, or a patch half-applied. This leads to partial-bypass scenarios.

Database migration scripts: If migrations run with elevated permissions, and you can influence the migration through an injection, you can escalate privileges directly on the database.

Revertibility fail: After a rollback, old vulnerable code runs again. Monitor version headers or JavaScript bundle fingerprints to detect if a known-vulnerable version has been restored. Strike while it’s back.

Containerization misconfig: Exposed Docker sockets or Kubernetes API can give you control over the entire cluster. Look for port 2375 (Docker) or 10250 (Kubelet) without authentication.

5. Post-Release Testing & Observation → Your Actions Are Being Logged (Or Not)
Insufficient logging = blind spot. Attackers love targets that don’t monitor. But for you, test if errors reveal too much—verbose stack traces with framework versions, file paths, or database queries. That’s free recon.

Error reporting services: If Rollbar or Sentry dashboards are unsecured, you can watch live errors, often containing session tokens or user input. Check /sentry/ or search for public DSN keys in JS files.

Penetration testing artifacts: Sometimes the pentest report itself leaks—search for PDFs with "penetration test" and the company name. It’s a ready-made exploit guide.

6. Dependency Management → Third-Party Weaknesses
Outdated libraries are hunter’s bread and butter. Fingerprint the tech stack (Wappalyzer, response headers, JS bundles) and match against CVE databases. A single unpatched lodash or fastify can lead to RCE.

Build tool chain: If the target uses Webpack and has source maps exposed (.js.map files), you get readable source. That reveals all module versions and custom logic, making vulnerability discovery trivial.

